{"type": "thinking", "text": "Whoa, \"Mark as paid\" caused a big data change: The 13 November Rent entry moved to today (Today section) with time 08:07 AM, and now Today total = $25 (Rent $20 + Coffee $5). The 13 November section now only has Groceries $40 (Total $40). Also a snackbar \"Marked as paid\".\n\nSo marking the recurring payment as paid logged it as an expense today, and removed it from yesterday. That's arguably a bug: marking yesterday's (13 Nov) recurring payment as paid today moves its date to today (08:07 AM), instead of keeping the original due date (13 November 12:00 PM). Also it removes it from the 13 Nov section entirely.\n\nHmm, but maybe the \"Mark as paid\" action is for upcoming payments and logs at the time of marking. The entry was under 13 November though. Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe the intended model: Recurring payments appear in history for the day they occur. The \"Mark as paid\" converts the pending recurring entry into a real expense at the current time. But it should keep the date it was due. Moving from 13 Nov to today (14 Nov) changes history. Is that expected? \"Mark as paid\" probably should record actual payment date = today. Hmm.\n\nAlso \"Marked as paid\" snackbar confirms.\n\nNow the history shows \"Today\" section with Rent and Coffee, and \"13 November\" with Groceries only. Total spent now $65? Wait Today total $25 + 13 Nov $40 = $65. Budget spent was $60; now $65. The recurring Rent moved to today, so total overall still 60 (40+20) + 5 = 65. Consistent.\n\nIs this a bug to report? The precondition said a recurring monthly Rent expense of 20 happened yesterday; it showed as an unpaid upcoming entry with \"Mark as paid\" even though it was already counted in spent. That's suspicious. Then marking paid relocates it to today. Hmm.\n\nLet me undo? There's no undo. I've changed data; that's fine for testing, but I should be careful about the preconditions for further tests. The Rent is now a paid expense today.\n\nI may have introduced a state change that complicates comparisons, but I observed the before/after.\n\nPotential bug candidates:\n- Recurring entry's \"Mark as paid\" action changes the expense's date from 13 Nov to today's date (14 Nov) and moves it to another date section. Expected: mark as paid should not change the occurrence date (or should keep it on 13 Nov). This breaks history accuracy.\n- Also, the recurring entry was shown as an \"upcoming\" pending payment even though it was already counted in the period spend; marking paid didn't change total spent? Actually total spent increased by 5 due to Coffee, not Rent (Rent was already counted). Hmm, before marking paid: spent $60 (Groceries 40 + Rent 20). After: $65 (Groceries 40 + Rent 20 + Coffee 5). So marking paid didn't double count Rent. Good.\n\nBut the date/time changed from 13 Nov 12:00 PM to today 08:07 AM. That's a real issue: the \"Mark as paid\" moved it. Also, does it now appear twice? No, moved.\n\nHmm wait, maybe the Rent entry I marked as paid was the *upcoming next month's* occurrence shown outside period? No, I tapped the one in the 13 November section (element 14 at y=1147). Yes.\n\nSo after marking paid, the entry relocated to Today. That's weird but maybe by design (\"mark paid now\" = pay now).\n\nLet me now examine the analytics to see if savings card updates consistently with $65 spent / $35 available.\n\nAlso note the \"Today\" section includes Rent at 08:07 AM, no \"Recurrent\" label anymore—it's a normal expense now. Good.\n\nLet me check the analytics now."}
