one-time (paid)\". Consistent.\n\nSo maybe the savings card is fine, or the bug is subtle. Let me compare to a prior expected behavior: \"the card is now built from the shared summary data.\" Perhaps previously the card showed \"Ideal savings\" as 20% of *remaining budget* ($4.40) vs now fixed $20? Hmm.\n\nHmm, maybe I should look at the \"Ideal savings: $20\" line: 20% of total budget. And \"$20 × 6 = $120\". Both consistent.\n\nLet me move on; maybe the bug will surface elsewhere (e.g., after changing budget or finalizing period). But the task areas are specific.\n\nLet me look more carefully at the \"upcoming recurring payments in history\" rendering. The entries show:\n- In the period: \"GymSub | Recurrent expense — 08:10 AM | $3\" (with Mark as paid).\n- Outside period: \"GymSub | Recurrent expense — 08:10 AM | $3\" and \"Rent | Recurrent expense — 12:00 PM | $20\", total $23.\n\nThe \"basic expense composable\" probably shows the reason, the subtitle (\"Recurrent expense — time\"), and amount. What could be wrong? Notice the subtitle format: \"Recurrent expense — 12:00 PM\". The em dash then time. For a normal expense, the subtitle is just \"12:00 PM\". So the recurring entry shows both. That seems fine.\n\nPotential bug: For upcoming recurring payments, the amount shown might be the *period's* amount but the date should be the due date; the time shown might be the time the recurring was created rather than the scheduled due time. For Rent: created yesterday at 12:00 PM (precondition seed), so time 12:00 PM = scheduled time. For GymSub: created at 08:10 AM today, due day 14 monthly at... the time is 08:10 AM (creation time), reasonable.\n\nHmm, what about the \"Rent\" entry in the 13 November section originally: It appeared under 13 November with \"Recurrent expense — 12:00 PM\" and a \"Mark as paid\" action, and it was counted in the period total. That means an unpaid upcoming payment was included in \"Total: $60\". Is that right? The precondition says the Rent expense of 20 happened yesterday. So it should be a paid expense, not an upcoming one with \"Mark as paid\". The fact that it shows \"Mark as paid\" suggests the recurring expense's current occurrence is treated as upcoming/unpaid even though dated yesterday. That's inconsistent: an occurrence dated in the past (13 Nov, yesterday) shouldn't still be \"upcoming\". And after marking paid, it moved to today. Hmm.\n\nBut maybe the seed data intentionally created a recurring expense \"yesterday\" that is due... Hmm.\n\nActually, maybe the \"Mark as paid\" is part of the \"upcoming recurring payments in history\" feature: recurring entries are shown in the date they're due; if unfilled, they're \"upcoming\" and can be marked paid. For Rent due 13 Nov (past), it should perhaps be marked paid already or show as overdue. The seed marked it as a recurring expense created yesterday but not paid. So the app shows it as payable. The precondition says \"there was a recurring monthly Rent expense of 20.00\" — meaning it happened. So perhaps the seed is fine and the app should show it as a real expense, not pending. But since the total counted it, the app treats it as spent.\n\nHmm, then what happens when you \"Mark as paid\"? It creates a new expense at today, replacing the pending entry. The total spent didn't increase (still 20). It moved date to today. That seems like a plausible bug: marking as paid should keep the original due date.\n\nBut is \"Mark as paid\" even part of the changed areas? \"Upcoming recurring payments in history: entries there are now built with the basic expense composable.\" Yes, the upcoming entries and their interactions are in scope.\n\nLet me test \"Mark as paid\" on the GymSub entry displayed *outside* the period. It should probably mark it paid for today? Or for the next occurrence? Hmm, risky. Let me instead test the \"Edit\" action on the recurring entry and inspect the recurrence dialog, then Cancel (reversal test).\n\nActually, there's a more