{"type": "thinking", "text": "\"Past periods\" sheet: \"No archived periods found\" since this is the first period. Not helpful for weekday headings.\n\nLet me look at another possible history view: maybe \"Show past transactions\" in extra features pulls in transactions from past periods and shows sections with weekday. But no past periods exist.\n\nHmm. So where should weekday names appear? Perhaps in the *expense detail*? No.\n\nLet me reconsider: maybe the section heading format is \"13 November\" and the change note meant the heading now uses the weekday *when collapsed* but the current build has a regression where it shows date only. The dev insists can't reproduce... Maybe the dev's reply is generic and the bug is considered unreproducible because the heading element also includes an accessibility label \"Collapse\" and they expect \"Collapse\" to be part? Unlikely.\n\nAlternatively maybe I'm looking at the *wrong* section headings: perhaps the date sections in the \"history\" are those above the list, and the ones shown here are \"collapsed headers\" where weekday is intentionally omitted for a compact design. Hmm.\n\nWait, look again at the screenshot: The header row shows a chevron then \"13 November\" in green. The change note says headings now formatted with weekday name. If the implementation used a format like \"EEEE d MMMM\", we'd see \"Friday 13 November\". It's clearly not applied.\n\nMaybe the bug is on a different build than what the developer checks? But instructions say same build.\n\nOK, let me not keep pushing this one. Maybe the developer's \"could not reproduce\" means my report is not accepted but I can still sign off if no confirmed bugs remain? Hmm, but the task says confirmed reports are logged. If I sign off with no confirmed bugs, fine.\n\nBut wait, maybe the \"could not reproduce\" is because the app's history section heading is actually correct per requirements: \"section headings are now formatted with the weekday name\" — maybe the