{"type": "thinking", "text": "Again rejected. Hmm, maybe the dev is testing without the specific data changes I made. But the missing due date is obvious in the seeded state too (the original outside Rent entry showed \"Recurrent expense — 12:00 PM\"). The dev couldn't repro. Maybe the app is supposed to show only the time there and it's not a bug.\n\nI'm now suspecting that the \"could not reproduce\" is the standard response for reports deemed not valid. Perhaps I'm misidentifying the bugs because they're in different spots.\n\nLet me think differently. Perhaps the three changed areas are implemented in a way where the bugs are subtle but verifiable *without* my data modifications. Let me reset the app data to the original preconditions? I can't reset data except by deleting my added expenses and maybe marking... The one-off state has changed (Rent moved to today). Hmm.\n\nCould I reset the app data via Settings? There's no reset. Maybe clearing app data isn't possible via the UI. The task says \"Do not change data outside what testing requires\" — I've changed a lot. That's okay for testing, but the original baseline is gone.\n\nHmm, maybe I should re-read the change notes and think about which *user-visible* things would be wrong:\n\n1. \"Analytics savings card: the card is now built from the shared summary data.\"\n   The savings card might display values from the *budget summary* which is period-wide, but the analytics screen has a *selected date/range* (Days/Week/Biweek). If the \"shared summary\" is the period summary while the rest of the analytics reflects the selected range, there could be a mismatch. Earlier, selecting 13 Nov (3/24) kept \"Total Spent $65\" and the savings card $35. Hmm, but maybe the *chart card* and stats changed? The chart/heatmap and \"Minimum/Maximum spent\" reflect the period. Hmm.\n\n   Wait — maybe the bug: the savings card shows \"Available: $35\" and \"Current savings 35%\" where these values are computed from the *shared summary* which includes *upcoming recurring payments* as spent, while the \"Ideal savings: $20\" is 20% of budget. That's consistent.\n\n   Maybe the bug: \"Current savings\" percentage doesn't match the \"Available: $X\" dollar value. Let me check: available $22, current savings 22%. Match. $35 → 35%. $40 → 40%. Always match. Fine.\n\n   Maybe the bug: \"Recurring expenses\" + \"One-time expenses\" percentages don't add to the total spent percentage? They did.\n\n   Maybe the bug is the *donut chart*: it shows categories (purchase reasons) rather than actual categories. But that's not the savings card.\n\n2. \"Upcoming recurring payments in history: entries there are now built with the basic expense composable.\"\n   Maybe the basic composable requires a category color/icon and the recurring entries have none, or the row height/layout is broken (text overlapping). No.\n\n   Maybe the upcoming recurring entries in history are *duplicated*: the same recurring occurrence appears both in the in-period section and in the outside-period list? Let's check the original state: Rent appeared in the 13 Nov section AND in the outside list (when toggled). Are those the same occurrence? The in-period Rent is due 13 Nov; the outside Rent is due 13 Dec. Different occurrences, but both display identical text (\"Rent / Recurrent expense — 12:00 PM / $20\") with no date. So the user can't tell them apart, and might think it's duplicated. Hmm! That's a *duplication* appearance bug caused by missing dates. In the original state, after toggling, the list showed the 13 Nov Rent and then below \"Rent / Recurrent expense — 12:00 PM / $20\" again — identical rows. That looks like a duplicate entry! And the totals: 13 Nov Total $60 includes Rent 20, and outside Total $20. A user would see Rent twice. This is a plausible bug: \"Rent appears twice with identical text (once in the 13 November section and once outside the period) with no date to disambiguate.\"\n\n   Hmm, but that's the same root cause (missing date).\n\n3. \"Expense history date sections: section headings are now formatted with the weekday name.\"\n   Missing weekday. Hmm.\n\nAll three suspected bugs get \"could not reproduce\". Maybe the developer's replies are not trustworthy or the exercise expects us to persist and eventually sign off? Or maybe there are *other* bugs in these areas that I haven't found, and the dev rejected my misidentified ones.\n\nLet me look at the app from scratch with a focus on \"what looks broken\". \n\nLet me re-check the history list rows for the \"basic expense composable\" change. Maybe the bug is that the *upcoming recurring entries* are rendered with the *wrong amount* or the *wrong reason*. In the original state: Rent $20 correct. Now: Rent $20, SkipTest $2, GymSub $3. Correct.\n\nMaybe the bug: the outside-period entries' *\"Recurrent expense —\"* subtitle uses an em dash and time, but for the *in-period pending* entries, the date section shows them under the wrong date? Hmm.\n\nWhat if the intended behavior is that upcoming recurring payments should be shown in a *separate* section (not mixed under \"13 November\"), and after Mark as paid they should be under today? Hmm.\n\nLet me look at the app's \"upcoming recurring payments in history\" more carefully: the in-period Rent entry under 13 November is not a transaction but a projection. Including it in `Total: $60` might double-count when the actual payment is logged later. We saw marking paid didn't increase the total (it converted). Fine.\n\nOK, new approach: let me test the *edit* of the recurring payment to see if the recurrence settings persist and whether the history entry updates. Perhaps the bug is there (state not persisted).\n\nI still have the SkipTest subscription (next Dec 14). It's outside the period. I can't click it in history. But maybe I can edit it via the Subscriptions screen — not accessible now. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, create a new in-period pending recurring (another), edit it, and check. Let me do a quick one: $1 recurring monthly, category Food, reason \"EditTest\". Then open history, expand EditTest, Edit, change amount to $4, save, and verify history shows $4. Also verify the Subscriptions screen \"Monthly commitment\" increases.\n\nActually, maybe the bug is in editing a recurring: the edit screen's \"Recurrent Expense\" toggle might be ON, but saving via the checkmark might create a duplicate or change the date. Let me test.\n\nBut I'm worried about churning data. Still, testing requires it.\n\nAlternatively, the bug might be in the *upcoming recurring entry's Edit* flow: the edit screen shows the date/time of the *next* occurrence? Hmm.\n\nLet me create EditTest $1 and check.\n\nActually, wait. Before more data changes, let me think about the \"Mark as paid\" behavior as the bug in the upcoming recurring payments area. The change note says entries are now built with the basic expense composable. Maybe the basic composable's click handler opens the *expense editor*, but for upcoming recurring entries the editor's \"Save\" would create a *new* one-off expense instead of updating the recurring payment. And \"Mark as paid\" is the correct flow. Hmm.\n\nOr maybe the bug: the upcoming entry when expanded shows \"Mark as paid\" but no \"Skip\" option, while the Subscriptions screen has Skip. Not a bug.\n\nLet me test the \"Edit\" action on a *pending in-period* recurring entry. First create one.\n\nSteps: Today keypad → 1 → recurrent toggle → category Food → reason \"EditTest\" → enter → monthly → save. Then history shows EditTest pending today. Tap it → Edit → change amount? Actually let me test the edit screen and cancel first (reversal), then edit amount and save, verify history/analytics.\n\nLet me do it. From current history screen, press BACK to keypad."}
