{"type": "thinking", "text": "Now with savings goal target $500 / 10 months:\n- \"To reach $500 in 10 months, save $50 per period.\" ✓ (500/10 = 50)\n- \"Ideal savings: $50\" (the target per period, 50% of the $100 budget)\n- \"Available: $0\" ✓\n- Current savings 0%.\n\nSo the goal is applied. The card is dynamic. That's good — no bug.\n\nNote: \"$50 per period\" — the period is 24 days (not monthly), and 10 months vs periods? Hmm, \"Target period (months)\" 10 months, but the app says \"save $50 per period\" where period = budget period (24 days). 10 months ≈ 12.5 periods of 24 days. So $500/10 = $50 per *month*, not per *period*. The label says \"per period\" but the goal uses months. Hmm! Potential bug: \"To reach $500 in 10 months, save $50 per period.\" If a period is 24 days, 10 months = ~12.7 periods, so per period should be ~$39.4. But it divided by 10 (months) and called it \"per period\". Hmm, though maybe the app treats \"period\" as a month here. Ambiguous.\n\nAlso the \"Ideal savings: $50\" now equals 50% of the $100 budget — because the goal target $500 / 10 months = $50/period. But with a $100 budget and 50/30/20, ideal savings should be $20. The card now shows $50 as \"Ideal savings\" — the goal overrides the split. Is that expected? Possibly the goal defines the ideal.\n\nThis goal feature seems to work. But there's the \"Ideal savings: $50\" vs the \"Current savings\" 0%. Fine.\n\nWait, the card's progress bar/ideal marker would be 50%. Fine.\n\nHmm, this is a lot of exploration with no confirmed bug.\n\nLet me revert the savings goal to blank (leave blank to keep default). I should clear both fields. Then maybe reset defaults [12] (Reset to defaults) in Settings would clear goal and set Balanced. Let me use \"Reset to defaults\" to clean up. That's cleaner. Go back to Settings and tap \"Reset to defaults\".\n\nActually, \"Reset to defaults\" might reset *all* savings preferences (split Balanced + blank goal). Good.\n\nLet me do that. Back to keypad [21], Settings, scroll, Reset to defaults."}
