{"type": "thinking", "text": "Reset to defaults cleared the savings goal fields (back to placeholders) and the split is Balanced. Good.\n\nNow, let me think about the savings card again: with the goal blank, the card shows the default \"$20 × 6 = $120\". Fine.\n\nI still haven't found a clear savings card bug.\n\nLet me revisit the *savings card duplicated \"Current savings\"* line. With the goal set to $50, the card showed \"Ideal savings: $50\" and \"Current savings 0%\"; the \"Current savings: $0\" line wasn't in that observe? It showed [14] Available: $0, [15] Ideal savings: $50, then [16] Recurring... — no \"Current savings: $0\" line! Wait, in the Aggressive saver state, there was a \"Current savings: $0\" line at y=1734. In the goal state, it wasn't listed. Hmm, maybe it was hidden behind the button (y around 2010) — the button at 2069. The observe listed Recurring at 2158, so the area between Ideal (1971) and Recurring (2158) is 187px; the \"Current savings: $0\" line would be there and should be listed, but wasn't. Hmm.\n\nSo the \"Current savings: $0\" line appears only when the split is Aggressive saver? That doesn't make sense. Maybe it's a conditional line shown when the *goal* is not set AND current < ideal? Or...\n\nActually, maybe the \"Current savings: $0\" line is a *separate element* that only appears when the savings percentage is 0 (red)? Hmm.\n\nWait, let me re-read the Aggressive state observe:\n[12] TextView \"Available: $0\" @(180,1695)\n[13] TextView \"Ideal savings: $40\" @(865,1695)\n[14] TextView \"Current savings: $0\" @(854,1734)\n[15] Recurring expenses @ 1867\nIn the goal state:\n[14] Available: $0 @(180,1971)\n[15] Ideal savings: $50 @(865,1971)\n[16] Recurring expenses @(299,2158)\nNo Current savings line. Difference: Aggressive saver (40/20/40) vs Balanced with goal. Hmm.\n\nMaybe the extra \"Current savings: $0\" line is shown because the target \"ideal savings\" ($40) is greater than available ($0), so the card shows the current savings amount too. With the goal $50 and available $0, the ideal $50 > available $0 too, so it should show. But it didn't. Unless the goal-based ideal is handled differently.\n\nHmm, maybe the \"Current savings: $0\" line is actually part of the progress bar legend that gets rendered only in certain layouts. This is too visual.\n\nActually, wait: in the Aggressive state screenshot, the labels under the bar were:\n\"Available: $0\" (left)\n\"Ideal savings: $40\" (right) — with a strikethrough? Looking at the screenshot, \"Ideal savings: $40\" appears with a horizontal line through it. And below it \"Current savings: $0\" in red.\n\nMaybe the \"Ideal savings: $40\" is *struck through* because the current savings is below ideal. Hmm, that's a design.\n\nIn the goal state, maybe \"Ideal savings: $50\" also struck and \"Current savings: $0\" below — but the observe didn't list it. Maybe it was hidden behind the floating button. The button is at y=2069 and covers 2019-2119; the \"Current savings: $0\" line would be at ~2010, just above the button... Hmm, in the Aggressive state, the labels at 1695/1734 were well above the button at 2069. In the goal state, the labels were at 1971 and the button at 2069; the extra line would be ~2010, overlapping the button → perhaps not in the accessibility tree? But the button is drawn over it; the element might still exist. Hmm, the observe didn't list it, so maybe the line is genuinely absent.\n\nThis is inconclusive.\n\nLet me consider that maybe the *default* state (with the seeded data) has a savings card bug that I dismissed: \"Current savings 40%\" — maybe it should be \"20%\" (the ideal)? No.\n\nHmm, hold on: maybe the \"Current savings\" is supposed to be the *dollar amount*, and the card shows \"40%\" (a percentage) — that's correct as a percentage. The small \"Current savings: $0\" adds the dollar amount when relevant. Hmm.\n\nOK, let me try a *fresh* angle: check the app's behavior when the budget is *edited*. The savings card is built from the shared summary; if I change the budget from $100 to, say, $200, the card should update (available = 200 - spent, ideal = 20% of 200 = $40, etc.). If it doesn't update, that's the bug. Budget editing is reversible. Let me test:\n- Change budget to $200 via budget sheet \"Edit Budget\".\n- Check savings card: available should be $200-$128 = $72 (36%), ideal $40, \"$40 × 6 = $240\".\n- Then revert budget to $100.\n\nThis is a good test of the \"shared summary\" integration. Let me do it.\n\nGo back to keypad, tap the date pill ($1.9 → now maybe banner), Edit Budget. Currently in Settings (scrolled). Tap back [13], then on keypad tap the date pill [1]. The date pill may be the \"Budget amount exceeded\" banner (since over budget). Tapping it earlier opened the budget sheet. Let me do it."}
